d i still believe  the establishment  ngos who work civil society works on  this i failed to get them to understand  but when i see the voices of the youth  today they understand they're rooted in  the politics of climate change and i'm  seeing a diversity of voices from the  youth as well so i think hats off to the  youth of this of the world today not  only are they talking truth to power but  they are  rooting the conversation on climate in  the reality of climate in the politics  of climate and that is critical for us  to move ahead so i i would only say do  more of what you're doing  thank you sunita and i believe that  question came from a yale undergrad so  thank you for that john any reaction to  that  well yeah i mean i think um uh younger  people have really set the um  established an initiative and  i can't ever claim to have been a  climate activist as such although i've  always been politically active and i  guess nowadays the two things have to  overlap to a very large extent  um but i think some of the movements  that one sees  such as this extinction rebellion  some of the other youth youth-based  movements but which have also attracted  a lot of support from  older generations  and which across social class as well  the people involved from working class  middle class  backgrounds um  of course different  different cultures define  social and cultural class in different  ways when one talks about middle class  in america it means something quite  different from when we talk about middle  class in england for example  nevertheless  the the activism i think spreads and  it's contagious and it's a good thing  and we have to accept that activism has  to be a broad church there are going to  be some aspects of it which we as  individuals might not like  um  but in other aspects which we feel are  not activist enough  but the crucial point is to make an  impact and to force  uh people to pay more attention to the  problems and issues that we face and  tha